==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|suspence||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|suspense}}, {{m|enm|suspence}}, from {{der|en|xno|suspens}} (as in {{m|fro|en suspens}}) and {{der|en|fro|suspens}}, from {{der|en|la|suspēnsus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-suspense.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{IPA|en|/səˈspɛns/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛns|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# The condition of being [[suspend]]ed; cessation for a time.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1717|title={{w|Eloisa to Abelard}}|authorlink=Alexander Pope|first=Alexander|last=Pope|lines=249–252|newversion=republished in|title2=The Complete Poetical Works of Alexander Pope|location2=Boston, New York|publisher2=Houghton, Mifflin and Company|year2=1902|page2=113|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/completepoetical00pope/page/113|passage=For thee the Fates, severely kind, ordain / A cool '''suspense''' from pleasure and from pain; / Thy life a long dead calm of fix'd repose; / No pulse that riots, and no blood that glows.}}
# the [[pleasurable]] [[emotion]] of [[anticipation]] and [[excitement]] regarding the [[outcome]] or [[climax]] of a [[book]], [[film]] etc.
# The [[unpleasant]] emotion of [[anxiety]] or [[apprehension]] in an [[uncertain]] situation.
#* {{RQ:Denham The Destruction of Troy}}
#*: Ten days the prophet in '''suspense''' remain'd.
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|pages=265–266|volume=I|passage=I believe that, to the young, '''suspense''' is the most intolerable suffering. Active misery always brings with it its own power of endurance.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} A temporary cessation of one's right; suspension, as when the rent or other profits of land cease by unity of possession of land and rent.
# {{lb|en|US|military}} A deadline.
#:''She sent us that assignment with a suspense of noon tomorrow.''

